## 1. Introduction
In prior decades, metal–organic frameworks (MOFs), constituted of metal-oxo nodes and organic linkers, have shown immense potential applications in gas storage and separation [1], pollutant adsorption [2,3], catalysis [4–6], sensing [7,8], and photocatalysis [9–14]. There is an unlimited possibility of combinations considering the diversified inorganic and organic building blocks of MOFs [15–18]. However, difficulty remains in precisely achieving the desired chemical functionality. It is worth pointing out that solvent is a significant factor in the synthesis of MOFs [19–21]. Thus, choosing an ideal solvent seems to be one of the primary options since most MOFs are synthesized by hydrothermal methods $[22-$ 25]. The solubility of metal salts and organic linkers in the solvent and the acid–base property and polarity of the solvent should be considered for the deprotonation and connection of the two building blocks [26–28]. In this context, various studies have been reported to explore the impact of solvent on the synthesis of MOFs. For example, a suite of solvents were screened to alter the exact shell parameters of MOF-5 [29]. Modulators and solvents were varied to generate changed morphologies and porous structures of NU-Zr-based MOFs [30]. The concentration of $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ in the other solvents was modified to synthesize different exposed facets of $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) [31] and different thickness of yolk-shelled ZIF${8@\mathrm{{mSiO}}_{2}}$ [32]. In parallel, efforts have also been put on adjusting the other conditions, including reaction temperature and time, as well as the kinds and amounts of modulators to prompt MOFs for better activity [33–37].
For photocatalysis, $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ or its derivatives have attracted the most attention since its inception [38–43]. With $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ -based materials as a blueprint, it is quite natural to envisage photocatalysis over MOFs built with Earth-abundant metal Ti nodes [44–48]. Since porphyrin has the strong absorption of visible light from 400 to $800\mathrm{nm}$ and the intense connection with Ti-oxo clusters for highly efficient charge transfer, enormous attention has been focused on the improvement of Ti-based porphyrinic MOFs (Ti-PMOFs). For example, PCN-22, a Ti-PMOF, was synthesized with demonstrable activity on selective photocatalytic oxidation of benzyl alcohol [49]. Furthermore, an analogous Ti-PMOF DGIST-1 with infinite chain structures was synthesized by replacing $\mathrm{Ti}_{6}0_{6}(0^{i}\mathrm{Pr})_{6}(\mathsf{a b z})_{6}$ cluster with $\mathrm{Ti}_{6}0_{6}(0^{i}\mathrm{Pr})_{6}(t-$ $\mathsf{B A})_{6}$ cluster and changing the concentration of ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{TCPP}}$ , which exhibited better photocatalytic activity than PCN-22 for the same reaction [50]. Recently, a novel ultrathin Ti-PMOF has been proven excellent photocatalytic activity of $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ evolution [51]. It is noteworthy that each Ti-oxo cluster is connected with two N,N0- diethylformamide (DEF) molecules by chemical bonding. Presumably, altering the solvent of synthesis can effectively reflect how solvent influences the structure and function of Ti-PMOFs.
To illustrate the effect of solvent on the photocatalytic activity of Ti-PMOFs, selective aerobic oxidation of amines irradiated by red light-emitting diodes (LEDs) was chosen as a representative reaction. In $N,N^{\prime}$ -dimethylacetamide (DMA) and DEF, Ti-PMOFDMA and Ti-PMOF-DEF were firstly synthesized. Thereafter, TiPMOF-DMA and Ti-PMOF-DEF were investigated by a series of characterizations, indicative of their effective formations. Notably, Ti-PMOF-DMA owned a bigger specific surface area and a larger pore volume than Ti-PMOF-DEF. Additionally, there were more abundant pores in Ti-PMOF-DMA than in Ti-PMOF-DEF, in favor of the photocatalytic conversion of benzyl amines. And Ti-PMOFDMA was exploited for the selective aerobic oxidation of benzyl amines irradiated by red LEDs, exhibiting superiority to Ti-PMOFDEF. A variety of amines were converted to the target imine products over Ti-PMOF-DMA with air as the oxidant. Furthermore, there was only slight loss of activity after three times of recycling for Ti-PMOF-DMA. A plausible mechanism centered on the primary reactive oxygen species (ROS) superoxide radical anion $(0\frac{\bullet-}{2})$ arrives based on the quenching results of ROS and in situ electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR) spectroscopy. The endeavor opens a chapter on the solvent-controlled synthesis of Ti-based MOFs in enhancing organic transformations.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Synthesis of Ti-PMOFs and Ti-oxo clusters
Ti-PMOF-DMA was synthesized according to a reported procedure [51] with some modifications. Firstly, through ultrasonication for $10\ \mathrm{min}$ , benzoic acid $(2700~\mathrm{mg},22.1~\mathrm{mmol})$ was dissolved in DMA $\mathrm{~\AA~mL}$ , $104~\mathrm{{mmol}};\$ ). With another $10~\mathrm{{min}}$ of ultrasonication, $4,4^{\prime},4^{\prime\prime},4^{\prime\prime\prime}$ -(porphine-5,10,15,20-tetrayl)tetrakis(benzoic acid) $(\mathsf{H}_{2^{-}}$ TCPP) $(50~\mathrm{mg},0.0633~\mathrm{mmol})$ ) was dispersed in the solvent. Finally, dropwise addition of tetrabutyl orthotitanate (TBOT) $(0.103\mathrm{\mL},$ ， $0.303~\mathrm{mmol}$ ) into the solvent was implemented. The mixture was transferred into a $50~\mathrm{mL}$ Teflon-lined hydrothermal autoclave after stirring for $30~\mathrm{min}$ . The autoclave was heated for $5{\textrm{d}}$ at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ After centrifugation $10000~\mathrm{rpm}$ for $5~\mathrm{min}$ ) and rinsing with acetone $\mathrm{\langle30mL\times3\rangle}$ , in a vacuum chamber, the final product was dried for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ at $60^{\circ}C$
Ti-PMOF-DEF was synthesized by simply altering solvent DMA with DEF (8 mL, 90.0 mmol). Moreover, the synthetic process of Ti-oxo clusters is similar to that of Ti-PMOF-DMA without adding 50 mg ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf T}{\sf C P P}$ $\left(0.0633\mathrm{\mmol}\right)_{.}$ .
## 2.2. The typical procedure for the oxidation of amines
First of all, Ti-PMOF-DMA $\mathrm{5~mg}$ was dispersed in acetonitrile $C H_{3}C N$ , 1 mL) in a $10\ \mathrm{mL}$ Pyrex-glass vessel containing amine $(0.3\mathrm{mmol})\cdot$ and chlorobenzene ( $10\upmu\mathrm{L}$ , the internal standard). After ultrasonication for $10\mathrm{min}$ , the vessel cap was punched to make air pass through and the vessel was placed under the irradiation of red LEDs $(623\pm8\ \mathrm{nm}$ with stirring at $1500~\mathrm{rpm}$ . The suspension was centrifuged to wipe off the photocatalyst and the supernatant was analyzed by gas chromatography equipped with a flame ionization detector (GC–FID). The products were certified by comparison with the retention time of the standard samples and further confirmed by gas chromatography–mass spectrometry (GC–MS). The quenching experiments were same with above operations except adding the quenching reagents to the Pyrex vessel before the ultrasonication. In the recycling experiments, the photocatalyst was centrifuged and washed with $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}$ several times for the next cycle. The conversions of the substrate and selectivities of the corresponding product were determined by GC–FID using chlorobenzene as the internal standard. The conversions of substrates and selectivities for the target products were calculated as follows: Conversion $(\%)=\left[\left(\mathsf{C}_{0}-\mathsf{C}_{\mathrm{r}}\right)/\mathsf{C}_{0}\right]\times100$ Selectivity $\begin{array}{r}{\left(\%\right){=}\big[\mathsf{C}_{\mathrm{p}}/(\mathsf{C}_{0}-\mathsf{C}_{\mathrm{r}})\big].}\end{array}$ - $\times100$ , where $C_{\mathrm{r}}$ and $C_{\mathsf{p}}$ are the concentrations of the substrate and the target product, respectively, at a certain point following the photocatalytic reaction, and ${\sf C}_{0}$ is the starting concentration of reactant.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Characterizations of Ti-PMOFs and preliminary results of photocatalysis
Ti-PMOF-DMA and Ti-PMOF-DEF show the weak and broad peaks around $2\theta=4.5^{\circ}$ in powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns (Fig. 1a and b), indexed as the facet of (002) in reference to the prior report [51]. The calculated d-spacing is 2.03 and $2.05\ \mathrm{nm}$ , respectively, which could be denoted as the interplanar distance of two neighboring porphyrin units. The adsorption and desorption isotherms measured with $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ at $77~\mathrm{K}$ and 1 atm are displayed in Fig. 1c and d. Two similar type IV isotherms demonstrate that altering the adsorbed solvent molecules barely changes the adsorption and desorption properties of the both MOFs. The Brunauer Emmett Teller (BET) specific surface area and pore volume of Ti-PMOF-DMA $(449.4~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $0.43\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}.$ are bigger than those of Ti-PMOF-DEF $(335.4~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $0.28~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}.$ ). The pore size distribution curves suggest that Ti-PMOF-DMA exhibits more abundant micropores and mesopores than Ti-PMOF-DEF (Fig. 1c and d, insets) using density functional theory (DFT) model. The mesopores are mostly originated from the incompact accumulation, entanglement, and imbrication of the Ti-PMOF. The basicity of DEF is stronger than that of DMA, which would lead to a higher deprotonation rate of ${\sf H}_{2}\mathrm{TCPP}$ than when DMA solvent was used. The higher deprotonation rate of the ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{TCPP}}$ would accelerate crystallization process, resulting in smaller crystals. The small size of Ti-PMOF-DEF accumulated more intensely due to higher combination rate of ${\sf H}_{2}\mathrm{TCPP}$ and Ti-oxo clusters, which resulted in a smaller specific surface area and pore volume. And about $71\%$ of $\mathsf{V}_{\mathrm{total}}$ of Ti-PMOF-DMA are contributed by mesopores (pore width $>2\ \mathrm{nm}$ ), ${\sim}5\%$ more than that of Ti-PMOF-DEF.
Next, the chemical states of elements of Ti-PMOF-DMA and TiPMOF-DEF were inspected by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) (Fig. 2). Both spectra of C 1s can emerge to three peaks of the binding energy of 288.7, 285.0, and 284.7 eV assigned to $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ , CAN, and ${\mathsf{C}}{=}{\mathsf{C}}/{\mathsf{C}}{-}{\mathsf{C}}$ bonds, respectively (Fig. 2a) [52]. In the meantime, in the spectra of O 1s (Fig. 2b), the peaks centered at 531.7 and $530.2\mathrm{eV}$ are ascribed to $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ and TiO bonds, indicating the existence of Ti-oxo clusters and porphyrinic ligands. The peak at $533.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ proves the existence of hydroxyl group $(-\mathsf{O H})$ . Additionally, the N 1s convolutions of Ti-PMOF-DMA and Ti-PMOFDEF were studied (Fig. 2c). The primary peak arranged at $400.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ is assigned to be $C{\mathrm{-N}}$ bonds. There is a small peak at around $397.9\mathrm{eV}$ , which could be the $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{=}\mathsf{N}$ bonds in ${\sf H}_{2}\mathrm{TCPP}$ . Notably, the relative contents of $C-N$ bonds in Ti-PMOF-DEF and TiPMOF-DMA are about $90\%$ and $85\%$ , respectively, which are larger than the theoretical values. Therefore, it may prove the presence of solvent molecules in these two Ti-PMOFs. The Ti $\mathsf{2p}$ spectra of TiPMOF-DMA and Ti-PMOF-DEF are revealed in Fig. 2d, certifying the similar state of Ti clusters.
