To date, the selective oxidation of amines is under intensive investigation since the product imines play a critical role in the synthesis of fine chemicals and pharmaceuticals [53–56]. Until now, a few MOFs have been demonstrated notable outcomes on the selective photocatalytic oxidation of amines [57–59], such as PCN-222 [60], PCN-822(Hf) [61], and $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) [62]. Nevertheless, most of these MOFs cannot respond to red light and should take a few hours to make the reaction accomplished. There is still ample room for exploring Ti-PMOFs to overcome the above shortcomings. Subsequently, selective photocatalytic oxidation of benzylamine with dioxygen $(0_{2})$ over Ti-PMOF-DMA and Ti-PMOFDEF irradiated by red LEDs was carried out (Table 1).
